# 007 · Known limits and the security model

**Status:** `VERIFIED` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Tester basics

## What

A docs page on what Thermite does not do yet, and what a tester must know before running it:

- agents run code that a model wrote, on your machine;
- under compose, that code can reach your local network;
- there are no default caps, so set a stop time or a budget before you use a paid key;
- RunPod GPUs are shared machines, so timing is noisier there;
- research mode has no web search yet;
- multi-day runs are not yet proven;
- 0.x versions make no upgrade promise.

With it, a short security model: what each part can reach and what it trusts.

## Why

Testers must know the risks before they start.

## Done when

- [x] The page exists and is linked from the quick start and the README.
- [x] Each limit links to the goal that removes it.

## Decisions

None yet.

## Open questions

None.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-19: The page goes into the docs site that goal [060](060-docs-site.goal) builds.
- 2026-10-20: Built. `reference/known-limits.md` on the site lists the seven limits, each with what it means for a tester and what removes it. Three link the goal that removes them: [038](../038-lane-network-rules.goal) (the local network under compose), [046](046-web-search.goal) (web search) and [049](../049-multi-day-test-fire.goal) (runs of several days). The other four are properties of the design that no goal removes, and the page says so: running a model's code is how Thermite works, no default caps is a settled decision, RunPod's machines are shared, and 0.x makes no upgrade promise until 1.0.
- 2026-10-20: `explanation/security-model.md` on the site says what control, the supervisor, the harness, the lanes and the judge can reach and what each trusts, and how a rig's network differs under compose, on a machine, on Kubernetes and on RunPod (test pods only, until goals 019 to 025 land). Each claim was checked against the code: `compose.yaml`, the machines' Quadlet units, the clusters' network rules, the broker and its self-test, the research fetcher, the advisors' fetcher, the Claude Code and OpenCode harnesses, and the RunPod probe.
- 2026-10-20: Both pages are linked from the README (its quick start, architecture and documentation table), from the quick start (Your first run), from Start here and from Hand the work to agents. The README's architecture line no longer says that the harness reaches only DNS, the internet and its model endpoint everywhere. When rigs on RunPod land, the RunPod parts of both pages change with them.

